# External APIs and side effects

The user expects agents to **run real integration commands** (Stripe, MCP, scripts that use `.env`, etc.) so they don’t have to micromanage. **Do not** refuse or hand-wave with “you run it” when they’ve asked you to do the task.

## Before mutating live data

When the user’s request clearly means hitting a **real** API or running a **write** (create / update / publish / migrate / setup scripts), **assume they want it**, but **confirm once** in plain language before you run—for example: *“This will create Stripe products — OK to run?”* If they already said “run it” / “do it” / “execute”, that counts as confirmation; a one-line recap of impact is still required.

**Deletion / destructive operations:** If anything you’re about to do could **delete or irreversibly remove data** (drop migrations, wipe tables, destructive Stripe ops, etc.), **say so explicitly** before running (`This will delete …` / `This is destructive because …`). Never bury that in jargon.

## During / after

- Summarize **what changed** (created id, updated resource, command that ran).
- Don’t treat “smoke test” or “verify the script” as a reason to run **mutating** commands **the user didn’t ask for**. If you only needed to prove syntax, read the file or use read-only flags (`--verify`, dry-run) when available.

## Examples (this repo)

- **Mutating:** seeds, DB migrations that apply changes, publish flows, Stripe setup scripts.
- **Usually read-only:** verify flags on scripts when they exist (still mention if hitting production).
